{"name":"Rice Paper","hex":"#fffcdb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rice paper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin, fragile paper made from parts of the rice plant, used in artificial flowers and watercolour art.","Any similar paper made from a plant other than rice, such as bamboo or mulberry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rice_paper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bánh tráng","blocks":[{"text":"Bánh tráng, a Vietnamese term, sometimes called rice paper wrappers, rice crepes, rice wafers or nem wrappers, are edible Vietnamese wrappers used in Vietnamese cuisine, primarily in finger foods and appetizers such as Vietnamese nem dishes. The term rice paper wrappers can sometimes be a misnomer, as some banh trang wrappers are made from rice flour supplemented with tapioca flour or sometimes replaced completely with tapioca starch. The roasted version is bánh tráng nướng."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Nems_wafer_DSC04723.JPG/330px-Nems_wafer_DSC04723.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng","wikidata":"Q3094709"},{"title":"Wafer paper","blocks":[{"text":"Wafer paper, also called edible paper or rice paper, is a very thin, edible sheet used in baking, confectionery and pharmaceuticals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Esspapier.jpg/330px-Esspapier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer_paper","wikidata":"Q12024380"},{"title":"Rice","blocks":[{"text":"Rice is a cereal grain and, in its domesticated form, is the staple food of over half of the world's population, particularly in Africa and Asia. Rice is the seed of the grass species Oryza sativa —or, less commonly, Oryza glaberrima. Asian rice was domesticated in China some 13,500 to 8,200 years ago; African rice was domesticated independently in West Africa about 3,000 years ago. Rice has become commonplace in many cultures worldwide; in 2023, 800 million tons were produced, placing it third after sugarcane and maize. Only some 8% of rice is traded internationally. China, India, and Indonesia are the largest consumers of rice. A substantial amount of the rice produced in developing nations is lost after harvest through factors such as poor transport and storage. Rice yields can be reduced by pests including insects, rodents, and birds, as well as by weeds, and by diseases such as rice blast. Traditional rice polycultures such as rice-duck farming, and modern integrated pest management seek to control damage from pests in a sustainable way."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg/330px-20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice","wikidata":"Q5090"},{"title":"Paper","blocks":[{"text":"Paper is a thin sheet of matted cellulose fibers. Largely derived from lignocellulose, paper is created from a pulp dissolved into a slurry that is drained and dried into sheets. Different types of paper are defined by constituent fiber, paper pulp, sizing, coating, paper size, paper density and grammage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG/330px-Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper","wikidata":"Q11472"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg/500px-Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg/1280px-Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg/500px-Spreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spreading and steaming rice paper","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpreading_and_steaming_rice_paper.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng_1.jpg/500px-B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng_1.jpg/500px-B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Banh trang production in Vietnam","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AB%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg/500px-Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg/1280px-Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg/500px-Rice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Batter and tools for making rice paper by hand","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARice_paper_batter_and_tools.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Pressing_rice_paper.jpg/500px-Pressing_rice_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Pressing_rice_paper.jpg/1280px-Pressing_ric